00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:48.340
for each month.

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:51.100
See if times get seasonal
affective disorder or something

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:52.353
like that.

00:09:52.353 --> 00:09:53.770
Winter months,
everything's like--

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:56.810
We get all
negative in January.

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:58.000
So I ended up using--

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.875
I was thinking about
how I wanted to do this.

00:09:59.875 --> 00:10:02.350
I arranged it, and then
I just did a slice max.

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:04.090
Nice.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:08.360
And then-- oh, yeah,
here's the basic table.

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:13.440
So you've got your month, your
word, and then your sentiment.

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:16.090
And so I thought that was kind
of fun, and then I was like,

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:17.810
let's make it look
a little nicer.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:21.250
So I went for a black and
white theme, like a newspaper.

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:22.730
That's nice.

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:23.740
Thank you.

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:25.390
What font is that?

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:27.150
This is Libre--

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:28.120
Libre Baskerville.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:30.078
Yeah, I was thinking of
using Baskerville, too.

00:10:30.078 --> 00:10:32.840
It seems like such a
great writerly font.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:33.340
Yeah.

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:35.590
So The New York Times, it
turns out, uses Georgia,

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:38.260
and Georgia is not
on Google Fonts.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:39.880
I think I have
Georgia, but I was

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:43.450
like, we'll do Google Fonts
so it's replicable for anybody

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:44.897
at home doing this.

00:10:44.897 --> 00:10:46.480
Yeah, Baskerville's
a total classic.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:48.220
I'm glad there's a
free version of that.

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:52.280
Yeah, so did that,
pretty happy with it.

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:54.430
I couldn't get--
like it bothers me

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:58.400
that this space is
bigger than this space.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:00.400
And so I spent some time
moving things around,

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:04.090
and just couldn't get
it where I wanted it.

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:06.430
It's tough because it
doesn't center nicely.

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:07.720
Then everything gets crowded.

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:08.590
It gets too thin.

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:09.690
I don't know, its--

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:11.810
Yeah, it was rough.

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:13.610
I made it work.

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:16.150
I thought it was interesting.

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:18.970
How did I-- what's the
best way to present things?

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:20.770
Because there were a
lot of ties, right?

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:27.490
So February has multiple ties
for its most positive words.

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:29.410
And then I think
there's something here

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:30.890
around the spread.

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:34.180
So almost always you
see your negative word.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:36.910
There's always a clear
negative winner and then

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:39.190
a spread of positive winners.

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:42.700
There's never really-- like
June, abolish and elite.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:44.830
There we go, done deal.

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:47.830
But there's-- oh, OK August,
you see a lot more negative.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:50.460
I don't know what's
happening in August.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:53.860
People are not as happy
in August, I guess.

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:55.860
Yeah, that's an angry month.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:56.360
Right?

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:57.590
That's what I'm seeing.

00:11:57.590 --> 00:11:58.360
Lots of anger.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:01.570
You're either idle
or you're irate, irate.

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:03.520
You're going to sack something.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:04.580
Loose, too.

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:05.080
Sue.

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:06.100
You get litigious, too.

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:07.200
Oh my God.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.250
Yeah, September
starts to balance out.

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:12.280
October, a little more
negative than positive.

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:13.780
So I thought this was--

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.810
and then I've got my little
source notes in here.

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:17.620
Oh, nice.

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:18.717
Yeah, I did not use--

00:12:18.717 --> 00:12:19.550
That was fantastic

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:21.190
--a [? blowing ?]
lowering the line.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:22.705
I thought of it later,
and I was like, it's OK.

00:12:22.705 --> 00:12:23.260
It's OK.

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:23.760
It's OK.

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:27.430
But I think, if
I were to redo this

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:29.150
or if I were to keep
working on this,

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:31.510
I was really curious
about doing something

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:34.450
where, instead of just
looking at the top,

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:38.680
it kind of did a full
sum of all the negatives,

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:40.480
and then a full sum
of all the positives,

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:43.640
and then it gave you a
positivity proportion.

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:45.460
So if it was--

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:47.530
let's take blue and orange.

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:49.630
If it's more positive,
it's more blue,

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:50.897
and it would somehow color--

00:12:50.897 --> 00:12:53.230
That would be interesting,
like the months themselves,

00:12:53.530 --> 00:12:54.030
Yeah.

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:56.740
So you could look at
January and be like, oh,

00:12:56.740 --> 00:12:59.110
that's really orange,
it's a really angry month,

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:01.908
whereas February is blue, and
you could see how that swings.

00:13:01.908 --> 00:13:04.450
So that, to me, was like-- that
would be a really interesting

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:05.860
programmatic next step.

00:13:05.860 --> 00:13:11.150
And then maybe give a sample of
the most negative words or most

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:11.650
positive.

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:12.550
Those are good ideas.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:13.925
I'm surprised
there's no neutral.

00:13:13.925 --> 00:13:15.240
I guess that's just the way.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:15.740
these--

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:18.290
So it's the sentiment
library that I used.

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:18.790
Oh, I see.

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:20.560
So this doesn't have
that, I guess, right?

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:21.060
Yeah.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:24.100
So this sentiment library--

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:28.060
and tidytext, Mining with
R-- it's a great book.

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:30.357
That goes into all of
the things that you

00:13:30.357 --> 00:13:32.440
need to think about when
you do this kind of work,

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:35.890
but it is just--

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:38.800
I think that it is
just positive or--

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:39.350
that's it.

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:40.690
It's a very dichotomous--

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:42.628
Yeah, that's right.

00:13:42.628 --> 00:13:44.920
Speaking of tables, I love
what you did with doing away

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:45.910
with the column labels.

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:47.110
It's totally not needed.

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:48.850
It's obvious what's
going on here.

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:52.721
And you just used
the group labels to--

00:13:52.721 --> 00:13:53.221
Yeah.

00:13:53.221 --> 00:13:54.221
--organize everything.

00:13:54.221 --> 00:13:55.120
I did that.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:59.380
That was-- Let me go in here.

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:02.910
I just dropped them by
putting them in these blanks.

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:03.705
Oh, perfect.

00:14:03.705 --> 00:14:05.830
That's good because it
leaves space, which is nice.

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:06.330
Yeah.

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:08.700
Yeah, so you get a
little space here.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:11.107
And then I thought about,
oh, like decorative borders.

00:14:11.107 --> 00:14:13.440
And I was like, no, it's
almost like a newspaper column.

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:14.160
Yeah.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:15.327
That's what I'm seeing here.

00:14:15.327 --> 00:14:17.150
That's the vibe I get, totally.

